WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.202
WIL: Mike, Matt,
congratulations.

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.971 line:0%
You've made it to the next phase
in this operation, which means

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:06.305 line:0%
we're sending you back
to your home forges

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:12.311 line:0%
to recreate this iconic
weapon, the United

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:15.181 line:0%
States Air Force
non-commissioned officer's

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:15.915 line:7%
sword.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.017
 [sigh]

00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:19.318
 You'll have four days
at your home forges

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:21.121
in which to complete
this challenge.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.323
At the end of four days, you'll
return and present your swords

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:25.124
to our panel of expert judges.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:26.860
And after they've put
them through the paces,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.695
they'll move one of you
one to our final Battle

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:33.099
of the Branches competition.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.401
You also need to make a
scabbard for the sword.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:36.569
 Oh, man.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:37.870
 I'm just messing with you.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:38.837
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:40.506
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:41.274
 Good luck, brother.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:45.979 line:7%
MIKE: It's day one.

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:48.080
It feels great to be back
here in my home forge.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.082
For this blade, I'm going
to be using Damascus.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:52.418
And I'm going to try to put
in a twist just for fun.

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:55.354
A lot of Air Force ties in
how I live my life day-to-day,

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:58.157
and this challenge
was no exception.

00:00:58.257 --> 00:00:59.959
There's a lot of firsts
for me on this blade.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.527
I've never done
Damascus this long.

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:03.396
I've never heat-treated
anything this long.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.531
I've never made a
crossguard like this before.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.300
It's going to be a
bit stressful for me.

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:11.670
None of my welds popped, so
at least it's a solid billet.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:15.040
So we won't know for sure
until we're grinding into it,

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.277
but I'm fairly certain it
was a successful twist.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:20.513
I don't think we
have any inclusions.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.982
I think it's going
to look pretty good.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:25.051
So I've got 16 layers
now in this billet.

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.586
I've got it drawn out enough.

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:29.255
I can cut it into three sections
and stack it back on itself

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.257
for a total of 48 layers.

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.125
This is going to be one of
the longest things I've ever

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:34.894
forged, so keeping
it straight might

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.229
be a little bit of a
challenge, especially

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.430
with the twist in it.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.031
That could cause
some things to warp.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.134
All in all, a solid
finish for day one.

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:43.636
[music playing]

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.239
MATT: Now that I'm one
of two [inaudible] left,

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.540
I definitely want
to be, you know,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.442
the one that shows
that the Air Force has

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:54.246
what it takes to win the "Forged
in Fire" branches competition.

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:59.719
The plan for today is to draw
the 1084 round stock out.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.855
I'm just going to draw the line
on the floor to give me an idea

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:04.891
of how far I've drawn it out.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.459
To start, the 1084
steel was 20 inches.

00:02:07.559 --> 00:02:09.762
I've got to draw it
out to about 35 inches

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:12.899
to get the crossguard, the
handle, and the pommel.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.635
I've still got to draw it
out quite a bit, get it down.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:19.372 line:0%
This 27- to 28-inch
sword, it's going to be

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:22.041 line:7%
interesting keep it straight.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.176 line:0%
It is at negative 18
outside right now,

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:27.313 line:0%
so I'm ready to get inside
and turn this forge on.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.983
So yesterday, I got
the blade drawn out.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.218
This morning, we're
going for the quench.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:35.421
Getting something this long
hot and keeping it straight

00:02:35.487 --> 00:02:40.059
is going to be one of my
biggest challenges right now.

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:42.495
Now, I've got a warping issue.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:44.396
I might be heat
treating it again.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.132
In the back of your mind,
you're always thinking,

00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:47.934
did the heat treat not go well?

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:52.004
Quenching two times, we'll have,
you know, bad grain structure.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:52.906
I have a ton of concerns.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.413 line:0%
Slight warp, but
at this point, I'm

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:03.883 line:7%
definitely not quenching again.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.351 line:0%
MIKE: It's the
morning of day four.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:06.819 line:7%
I didn't sleep very well.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.221
I still need to
finish the pommel.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.488
I need to finish the crossguard.

00:03:09.588 --> 00:03:11.591
And I haven't even
started the handle yet.

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:14.694
I can tell we're
almost through, though.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.162
Almost there.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.130
I can feel it.

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.565
I definitely think
this is a blade

00:03:19.631 --> 00:03:21.234
worthy of having me
stand at the Air Force

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.369
and go on to the final round.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.671
It is an example of
how I problem-solve,

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.308
how I look for solutions,
and how I don't give up.

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.111
All right.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:34.746 line:0%
That's as good as
we're going to get.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.115 line:7%
MATT: Day four.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.250 line:0%
And today, I need to get
the crossguard fitted up,

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:40.386 line:0%
the handle shape
wrapped with wire,

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:43.589 line:0%
and then the pommel
assembled as well.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:44.590
[inaudible]

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.458
Looking at the
original NCO sword,

00:03:46.558 --> 00:03:48.528
I was trying to figure out
what the best way to make

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.629
that wire-wrapped handle was.

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:51.464
Safety wire.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.698
We used that in the military.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.066
I thought that was
good to kind of throw

00:03:54.132 --> 00:03:56.101
a little Air Force-ism into it.

00:03:56.201 --> 00:03:57.604
It works perfect.

00:03:57.704 --> 00:03:59.172
I'm definitely nervous that
I have only one day left.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.639
In the back of your mind,
you're always thinking,

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.942
do I have what it takes
to move on to the Battle

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.076
of the Branches?

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:05.945
I mean, that's what
it's all about.

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.046
That's it.

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:10.082
May the best airman win.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.317
DOUG: All right, airmen.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.718
Now, I'll take your
weapon and deliver

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.355
some thrusts and slashes
on this ballistics dummy.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.322
Mike, you're up first.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:18.023
You ready?
- Absolutely.

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:18.791
Let's do it.

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.681
[laughter]

00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:46.218
 All right, Mike.

00:04:46.318 --> 00:04:47.687
It's a very light sword.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.089
Because of that, you
can move with it.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:53.292
Now, your tip allows
for a nice thrust.

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.061
With your edge, it slices.

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:57.062
I can feel every cut in there.

00:04:57.162 --> 00:05:00.032
But more importantly,
it will kill.

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:01.133
- Thank you.
- All right, Matt.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:01.867
Your turn.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:02.735
You ready, sir?

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:03.436
 Yes, sir.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:22.989
 [sigh]

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:25.325
[chuckling]

00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:27.026
 All right, Matt.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.628
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.463
The edge that you have
here is very sharp.

00:05:31.563 --> 00:05:34.500
The point punctures nicely.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.570
And most importantly,
sir, it will kill.

00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:38.103
 Awesome.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:38.837
Thank you.

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:39.872
 All right.

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.440
Next up, the strength test.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.944
For that, we have Ben.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:46.311
 Hello, bladesmiths.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.547
To test the strength and
durability of your swords

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:48.814
as well as their
overall construction,

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:52.618
I'll be bashing them
into this propeller.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:53.986
Mike, you're up first.
You ready?

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:55.254
 No.

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:57.289
 [laughter] We're
going to do it anyway.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:05:59.992
 All right.

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:02.962
[pounding]

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:04.764
 [sigh]

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:08.568
[pounding]

00:06:16.975 --> 00:06:18.511
[clattering]

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.347
 Good job.

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:22.715
 Oh, my gosh.

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:25.484
 Well, Mike, your
blade help up great.

00:06:25.584 --> 00:06:28.087
The only issue I have with
it is that I would really

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:29.421
like this pommel to be bigger.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.924
If the pommel were bigger,
it would give me a stop.

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:34.093
But it's got a nice weight.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.861
It's still sharp
as when it started.

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:36.862
Really well done.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:37.930
 Thank you so much.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:39.164
 Matt, you're up.
What are you thinking?

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:39.932
 I'm ready.

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:46.172
[pounding]

00:07:00.152 --> 00:07:01.086
[clattering]

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.422
 That's badass.

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:06.892
 Well, Matt, your
blade held up great.

00:07:06.992 --> 00:07:08.194
It's still sharp.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.795
This is a much heavier sword.

00:07:09.861 --> 00:07:13.999
But the biggest issue I have
is with the crossguard in back.

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:16.201
Your crossguard is loose.

00:07:16.301 --> 00:07:17.402
It wiggles.

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:19.605
And also, your
wire wrap is loose.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:22.341
But it's all in one piece.

00:07:22.441 --> 00:07:23.942
It did a heck of a
thing on that propeller.

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:24.710
Well done.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:25.544
 Thank you.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:26.545
BEN: But we're not finished yet.

00:07:26.611 --> 00:07:27.646
Next up is the sharpness test.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:28.648
And for that, I
give you to Doug.

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:33.953
 All right, airmen.

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:35.621
To test the edge of your
blade, I'm going to be

00:07:35.687 --> 00:07:38.590
cutting across these harnesses.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.026
I'm looking for clean cuts.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:41.861
Mike, you're up first.

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:42.428
You ready for this?

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:43.295
 Let's do it.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:43.996
 Let's do this.

00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:02.515
- Good job.
- Right through it.

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:03.315
 That was good.

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:04.116
Sharp.

00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:05.718
 All right, Mike.

00:08:05.784 --> 00:08:08.721
Light in flight and sharp enough
to cut through the harness.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:09.321
It will cut.

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:10.389
 Thank you, sir.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.156
 Good job.

00:08:11.257 --> 00:08:12.625
 Thanks, man.

00:08:12.692 --> 00:08:13.993
 All right, Matt.
Your turn, sir.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:14.827
You ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:26.005
Damn.

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:30.375
DOUG: All right, Matt.

00:08:30.442 --> 00:08:31.577
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:33.111
It's kind of heavy.

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:36.815
It's so forward in its weight
that on this slash, using

00:08:36.882 --> 00:08:39.318
the last six inches,
it just scratched

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:40.886
the back of the harness.

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:42.988
But on the backhand swing, where
it's going all the way through,

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:44.422
it cut the harness.

00:08:44.523 --> 00:08:46.191
But your weapon,
sir, it will cut.

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:46.925
 Thanks.

00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:47.759
 Good job, man.

00:08:47.826 --> 00:08:48.994
 Thanks.

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:54.332
 Mike, Matt, the bladesmith
moving forward is Mike.

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:56.067
Congratulations.

00:08:56.134 --> 00:08:57.736
 Thank you.

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.838
 Matt, first of all,
thank you for your service.

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:01.907
You have a beautiful
sword that did quite well.

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.842
But we based our
decision on the fact

00:09:03.909 --> 00:09:06.545
that your guard came loose,
the weight of your sword,

00:09:06.611 --> 00:09:09.315
and the wire wrapping
is also loose.

00:09:09.415 --> 00:09:11.250
That's the reason
we're sending you home.

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:12.451
 Yes, sir.

00:09:12.518 --> 00:09:14.587
 Matt, at this time,
I have to ask you

00:09:14.653 --> 00:09:17.056
to please surrender your blade.

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:18.657
MATT (VOICEOVER): I feel
good no matter what.

00:09:18.723 --> 00:09:20.225
I mean, I'm glad I made it here.

00:09:20.326 --> 00:09:21.059
DOUG: Good job.

00:09:21.159 --> 00:09:22.128
 Thank you.

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:24.729
I'm proud of myself for sure.

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:26.232
Mike's going to represent
the Air Force well.

00:09:26.298 --> 00:09:28.467
He's got a lot of people
rooting for him, me, as well.

00:09:28.567 --> 00:09:30.570
And he's a good
bladesmith, so good luck.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.843
 Mike, they said you're only
as good as your competition,

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:40.446
and you overcame a
fantastic competitor

00:09:40.513 --> 00:09:41.814
to move forward in
this competition

00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:45.151
to represent the entire
branch of the Air Force

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:47.252
at our Battle of the
Branches competition.

00:09:47.353 --> 00:09:48.087
How do you feel?

00:09:48.153 --> 00:09:49.388
 It feels amazing.

00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:51.724
 Please present your
blade to the judges.

00:09:51.791 --> 00:09:52.791
MIKE (VOICEOVER):
I can't believe

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:54.226
I'm the Air Force champion.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:56.127
I'm looking forward to
showing the rest of the DOD

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:57.530
what we've got.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:58.897
There's a lot of added
pressure moving forward.

00:09:58.964 --> 00:10:00.499
Now, I'm competing against
the Army, the Navy,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.167
and the Marine Corps, and
we've got a little bit

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:03.669
of a rivalry going on.

00:10:03.735 --> 00:10:06.672
So you better bring it,
because you know I'm going to.

